// Tier 1, chapter 1: kinematics. Words only; the numbers are generated per save.
// Every line of text here follows docs/writing.md: Lumen's lines in its own voice, the handbook pages
// as a textbook, the briefs as plain instructions. `cargo test` checks the rules it can.
Chapter(
    id: "1.1",
    tier: 1,
    domain: Mechanics,
    title: "The Helm",
    part: "Skiff's helm",
    intro: "The builders' skiff lies asleep on its slipway on the north shore, facing the strait. Its helm isn't aboard. It sits in a stone casket on a launcher at the canyon's rim, as if the road was sending it there when everything stopped. The wreck came down across that road at its south end. The road is pale stone that nobody here built, and its builders' machines stand along it: a cart in a groove, a crawler, a drop tower, two launchers and a quay. Every one of them is asleep, and each has its own console. Wake one and its console does what you set and nothing else. Do its job right and the next one wakes.",
    goal: "Get the skiff's helm to the north quay, up the road, across the canyon and over the ridge.",
    // Lumen's words. Spoken, so no digits; `look` is a place its eye holds while it speaks.
    opening: [
        Spoken(text: "Hello. I'm Lumen, the ship's computer, or a backup of it anyway, running on this section's cells. I can see fine, but I've lost my hands. I'm afraid you'll have to be them."),
        Spoken(text: "See that, across the strait? That's the main hull. The rest of me is in there, along with everything we'd need to leave. I keep calling it, and nothing answers yet.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait")),
        Spoken(text: "We came down right across a road. It's pale stone with quartz in the joints, and there are machines all along it, every one of them asleep. Nobody we know built them, and they're older than anything in my records. The console by their cart is still glowing, though."),
        Spoken(text: "Up at the north end there's a boat of theirs on a slipway. Its helm is sitting in a casket on a launcher over the canyon, as if they were in the middle of sending it when everything stopped. If their machines still work, I think we can help them finish the job.", look: Some("The skiff on the slipway")),
    ],
    closing: [
        Spoken(text: "Come and see the skiff. Its helm is in, and it's glowing.", look: Some("The skiff on the slipway")),
        Spoken(text: "The gate on the quay is pointing straight across the strait. And just now, for a moment, something answered me. It was faint, but it was the core, I'm sure of it.", look: Some("The north gate")),
    ],
    remarks: [
        Remark(near: "The wreck", radius: 7.0, when: Always, line: Spoken(text: "That's the forward section, or what's left of it. I'm running on its cells. I'm sorry we landed on their road. I hope they wouldn't mind.")),
        Remark(near: "The shore", radius: 10.0, when: Before("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "Still nothing from over there. Either the core's gone cold or my receiver has. I'd rather it was the receiver.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
        Remark(near: "The shore", radius: 10.0, when: After("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "The gate's still pointing over there. I've no idea what the builders meant by it, but I like where it's pointing.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
        Remark(near: "Ledge launcher: release point", radius: 8.0, when: Before("1.1.3"), line: Spoken(text: "That's a long way down. Nobody's carrying a casket across that, and whoever built this launcher must have thought the same.")),
        Remark(near: "Ridge: foot, near side", radius: 8.0, when: Before("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "The skiff's just over this ridge, and the strait right behind it. It's close enough to see, but far too steep to carry anything up.")),
        Remark(near: "The skiff on the slipway", radius: 12.0, when: Before("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "So that's their skiff. It's stone, all of it. I wouldn't have bet on stone floating, but they built it a slipway, so they must have known something I don't. There's an empty socket at the stern where the helm goes.")),
        Remark(near: "The skiff on the slipway", radius: 12.0, when: After("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "The helm's lit, but the rest of her is still dark. She'll need power, a sound hull and something to push her before she can cross. The rest of me will have to wait a little longer.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
    ],
    concepts: [
        Concept(
            id: "units",
            title: "Measurements carry units",
            found: FirstCommit("1.1.0"),
            hint: "Enter a value at the supply rail station and commit it.",
            card: "A measurement is a number together with a unit. 12 m is a length and 1.5 s is a time, but the number 12 on its own could be either.\n\nEvery value you enter at a station needs the kind of unit the station asks for. You can type the unit after the number, as in 12 m, or use a notebook reading by its name, such as d1. The notebook keeps each reading's unit with its number.\n\nIf a value has the wrong kind of unit, the station refuses it before anything runs, and a refusal doesn't use up a charge.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: Tape,
                caption: "On Earth, measuring a sofa with a tape. \"2\" on its own could mean anything, but \"2 m\" tells you it's a length.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Which of these is a time?",
                    choices: ["12 m", "12", "1.5 s", "3 m/s"],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "s stands for seconds, a unit of time. m is metres (a length), m/s is a speed, and 12 on its own isn't a measurement of anything.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A station asks for a length and you type 12. What happens?",
                    choices: ["It's read as 12 m.", "It's refused, because 12 could be anything.", "It's read as 12 cm."],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "A bare number has no kind, so the station refuses it, tells you why and uses no charge. Type 12 m, or a notebook name such as d1.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A road sign says the next town is 3 km away. In metres, that is…",
                    choices: ["3000 m", "300 m", "0.003 m"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "A kilometre is a thousand metres, so 3 × 1000 m = 3000 m. The length stays the same. Only the unit you count it in changes.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "velocity",
            title: "Velocity",
            found: Demo("1.1.1"),
            hint: "Watch the crawler and the haul cart set off. Try the experiment at the Catch the crawler station.",
            card: "Velocity tells you how fast a position changes, and in which direction. Over a stretch of steady motion, it's the distance covered divided by the time taken:\n\n    v = (change in x) / (change in t)\n\nIts unit is a length divided by a time, such as m/s. An object at a steady velocity covers equal distances in equal times, so its position grows in equal steps:\n\n    x = x0 + v t\n\nHere x0 is where it was when the clock started.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: Cyclist,
                caption: "On Earth, a cyclist riding at a steady pace, photographed once a second. The marks are evenly spaced, because the cyclist covers the same distance every second.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A cyclist covers 100 m in 20 s at a steady pace. Their velocity is…",
                    choices: ["20 m/s", "5 m/s", "2000 m/s"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "Divide the distance by the time: 100 m / 20 s = 5 m/s. Multiplying them would give m·s, which isn't a speed at all.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Photos taken once a second show a runner's positions evenly spaced. The runner is…",
                    choices: ["speeding up.", "slowing down.", "moving at a steady velocity."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Equal distances in equal times mean a steady velocity. If the gaps grew, the runner would be speeding up, and if they shrank, slowing down.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A train runs at a steady 30 m/s. It passes the 100 m mark as the clock starts. Where is it 4 s later?",
                    choices: ["At 220 m", "At 130 m", "At 120 m"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "x = x0 + v t = 100 m + 30 m/s × 4 s = 100 m + 120 m = 220 m. Remember to add where the train started.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "relative_velocity",
            title: "Relative velocity",
            found: Demo("1.1.1"),
            hint: "Watch the crawler and the haul cart set off. Try the experiment at the Catch the crawler station.",
            card: "Suppose two objects move the same way along a line at steady velocities v1 and v2. The gap between them changes at the difference of their velocities:\n\n    v1 - v2\n\nThis is the velocity of one as seen from the other. The gap changes by the same amount every second. It shrinks if the one behind is faster, and grows if the one in front is faster. When two objects move towards each other, they close the gap at v1 + v2.\n\nIf a chaser moving at v1 starts a distance D behind something moving at v2, it catches up after a time\n\n    t = D / (v1 - v2)",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: Overtake,
                caption: "On Earth, a car catching up with a bus. The gap between them shrinks by the same amount every second.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car at 25 m/s follows a bus at 15 m/s, both going the same way. The gap between them shrinks by…",
                    choices: ["40 m every second", "25 m every second", "10 m every second"],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "They're going the same way, so the gap changes at the difference of their velocities: 25 m/s − 15 m/s = 10 m/s.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Two cyclists ride towards each other at 6 m/s and 4 m/s. The gap between them closes at…",
                    choices: ["10 m/s", "2 m/s", "24 m/s"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "When they ride towards each other, both of them eat into the gap: 6 m/s + 4 m/s = 10 m/s.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car 50 m behind a bus closes the gap at 10 m/s. It reaches the bus after…",
                    choices: ["500 s", "5 s", "0.2 s"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "t = D / (v1 − v2) = 50 m / 10 m/s = 5 s. You can check the units too: m divided by m/s gives s.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "acceleration",
            title: "Acceleration",
            found: Demo("1.1.2"),
            hint: "Watch the test mass fall from the drop tower. Try the experiment at the Local gravity station.",
            card: "Acceleration tells you how fast a velocity changes. For an object speeding up steadily, it's the change in velocity divided by the time taken:\n\n    a = (change in v) / (change in t)\n\nIts unit is a velocity divided by a time, (m/s)/s, which is written m/s². An acceleration of 3 m/s² adds 3 m/s to the speed every second.\n\nIf an object starts from rest with a steady acceleration a, then after a time t its velocity and the distance it has covered are\n\n    v = a t\n    x = (1/2) a t^2\n\nBecause the speed grows by the same step every second, the object covers more ground in each second than in the one before. Photos taken at equal moments show the gaps getting longer.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: GreenLight,
                caption: "On Earth, a car pulling away from traffic lights, photographed once a second. Its speed grows by the same step every second, so each gap is longer than the last.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car pulls away from traffic lights. Read once a second, its speed is 0, 3, 6, then 9 m/s. Its acceleration is…",
                    choices: ["3 m/s²", "9 m/s²", "18 m/s²"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "The speed rises by 3 m/s every second, so a = 3 m/s / 1 s = 3 m/s². 9 m/s is how fast the car ends up going, which isn't the same as how quickly its speed changes.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A cyclist speeds up steadily from 2 m/s to 8 m/s in 3 s. Their acceleration is…",
                    choices: ["2 m/s²", "6 m/s²", "18 m/s²"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "a = (change in v) / (change in t) = (8 m/s − 2 m/s) / 3 s = 6 m/s / 3 s = 2 m/s².",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car starts from rest with a steady acceleration of 2 m/s². How far has it gone after 5 s?",
                    choices: ["10 m", "25 m", "50 m"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "x = (1/2) a t² = 0.5 × 2 m/s² × (5 s)² = 25 m. By then its speed is a t = 10 m/s, but it started from rest and wasn't going that fast the whole time.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "free_fall",
            title: "Free fall",
            found: Demo("1.1.2"),
            hint: "Watch the test mass fall from the drop tower. Try the experiment at the Local gravity station.",
            card: "When you let go of an object from rest and only gravity pulls on it, it falls with a steady acceleration called g, the gravitational acceleration, measured in m/s². After a time t it has fallen\n\n    h = (1/2) g t^2\n\nThe object's mass doesn't appear in this formula, so heavy and light things fall together. Air can hold back light, spread-out things such as feathers, but it hardly affects a crate or an apple.\n\nThe value of g depends on the planet. The value you learned was for a different planet, so you'll have to measure it here.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: AppleDrop,
                caption: "On Earth, an apple dropped from a balcony and photographed at equal moments falls further each time. In a tube with the air pumped out, a feather keeps up with a hammer.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Photos taken at equal moments show a falling apple. The gaps between its positions…",
                    choices: ["stay the same.", "grow as it falls.", "shrink as it falls."],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "The apple gains speed all the way down, so in each moment it falls further than in the one before.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "In a tube with the air pumped out, a feather and a hammer are let go together. Which lands first?",
                    choices: ["The hammer.", "The feather.", "They land together."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Mass doesn't appear in h = (1/2) g t². Only air holds the feather back, and the tube has none. Astronauts tried this on the Moon.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "On Earth, g is about 9.8 m/s². How far does a stone fall from rest in 2 s?",
                    choices: ["About 20 m", "About 10 m", "About 40 m"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "h = (1/2) g t² = 0.5 × 9.8 m/s² × (2 s)² = 0.5 × 9.8 × 4 m ≈ 19.6 m. On this planet g is different, so you'll have to measure it.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "independence",
            title: "Horizontal and vertical motion are independent",
            found: Demo("1.1.3"),
            hint: "Watch the two test blocks leave the ledge. Try the experiment at the Across the canyon station.",
            card: "A thrown object moves sideways and up and down at the same time, and the two motions don't affect each other.\n\nNothing pushes it sideways, so its horizontal speed stays the same:\n\n    x = vx t\n\nVertically, gravity acts on it just as in free fall:\n\n    y = vy t - (1/2) g t^2\n\nThis means that a block fired horizontally off a ledge reaches the ground at the same moment as a block simply dropped from the same ledge.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: TableEdge,
                caption: "On Earth, a marble rolled off a table and another dropped from its edge. At every flash the two are at the same height, and they land together.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A marble rolls off a table at the moment a second marble is dropped from its edge. Which lands first?",
                    choices: ["Both together", "The rolled one", "The dropped one"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "Rolling sideways doesn't give the marble any downward speed. Both marbles start falling from rest at the same height, so they fall for the same time.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "While the rolled marble is in the air, its horizontal speed…",
                    choices: ["grows.", "shrinks to nothing by the floor.", "stays the same."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Nothing pushes it sideways, so its horizontal speed stays constant: x = vx t. Gravity only changes the vertical part.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A ball rolls off a table at 2 m/s and is in the air for 0.5 s. How far from the table's edge does it land?",
                    choices: ["2 m", "1 m", "4 m"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "Sideways, x = vx t = 2 m/s × 0.5 s = 1 m. How long it stays in the air depends only on the table's height.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "components",
            title: "Splitting a launch into components",
            found: Demo("1.1.4"),
            hint: "Watch the two test slugs. Try the experiment at the Over the ridge station.",
            card: "If you launch something at speed v and angle theta above the horizontal, its velocity has a sideways part and an upward part:\n\n    vx = v cos(theta)        vy = v sin(theta)\n\nFrom then on the two parts move independently. The steeper the launch, the more of the speed goes into height and time in the air, and the less goes into covering ground.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: KickSplit,
                caption: "On Earth, a football kick. Its velocity splits into a sideways part, which stays the same, and an upward part, which gravity slows down and then turns around.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A football is kicked at the same speed but more steeply. Its upward part vy…",
                    choices: ["shrinks, and its sideways part grows.", "stays the same.", "grows, and its sideways part shrinks."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "A steeper kick sends more of the speed upwards, because sin(theta) grows with the angle while cos(theta) shrinks.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A ball is thrown straight up, at 90 degrees. Its sideways part vx is…",
                    choices: ["zero.", "equal to its speed.", "half its speed."],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "cos(90°) = 0, so none of the speed goes sideways and the ball comes back down where it started.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A kick at 20 m/s, 30 degrees above the ground. Its upward part is… (sin 30° = 0.5)",
                    choices: ["20 m/s", "10 m/s", "40 m/s"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "vy = v sin(theta) = 20 m/s × 0.5 = 10 m/s. The sideways part is 20 m/s × cos(30°), about 17.3 m/s.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "range",
            title: "Range on level ground",
            found: Demo("1.1.4"),
            hint: "Watch the two test slugs. Try the experiment at the Over the ridge station.",
            card: "If an object lands at the same height it was launched from, it stays in the air for\n\n    t = 2 v sin(theta) / g\n\nand in that time it travels a distance\n\n    R = v^2 sin(2 theta) / g\n\nTwo different angles give the same range, a flat one and a steep one the same number of degrees either side of 45. Their paths are different, though. The steep shot climbs higher and stays up longer.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: TwinKicks,
                caption: "On Earth, two kicks at the same speed, one flat and one steep, land on the same spot. The steep one goes higher and takes longer.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "On level ground, at the same speed, which angle sends a ball furthest?",
                    choices: ["30 degrees", "45 degrees", "60 degrees"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "R = v² sin(2 theta) / g is largest when sin(2 theta) = 1. That happens when 2 theta = 90°, so theta = 45°.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A kick at 20 degrees lands on a spot. At the same speed, which other angle lands there too?",
                    choices: ["40 degrees", "25 degrees", "70 degrees"],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "20 degrees is 25 below 45, and 70 is 25 above it. You can check: sin(2 × 20°) = sin(40°) = sin(140°) = sin(2 × 70°).",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Of the two kicks at 30 and 60 degrees that land on the same spot, which is in the air longer?",
                    choices: ["The 60 degree kick", "The 30 degree kick", "Both the same"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "t = 2 v sin(theta) / g. The steeper kick has more upward speed, so it climbs higher and stays up longer, though it covers less ground each second.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
    puzzles: [
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.0",
            kind: Rail,
            title: "The supply rail",
            task: "Load the rail cart and send it to the orange marker.",
            why: "This is their cart, and that's the one console that still glows. Nothing difficult here. It's a good place to see how every station works.",
            done: "The marker lit up as soon as the cart stopped on it, and then the stone gate opened by itself. Let's go and see what's past it.",
            brief: "Every job on this island runs through a station like this one. You read what's needed, measure the world, enter your values and commit, then watch what happens.\n\nThis first one needs no physics. The road the wreck came down across was built by someone else, and their machines stand along it, asleep. The first is a cart on a rail sunk into the road, running towards the outcrop. This is the supply rail. Its console, a stone of theirs beside the rail, still glows faintly. Through it the cart goes as far as you set and then stops, and the stone gate at the end of the stretch would stop it anyway. Load the cart, then send it to the orange marker across the rail.\n\nThe cart won't run empty. Beside the rail lies a block of the builders' stone, the size of the cart's bed. Leave the console, walk up to the block and pick it up with F, then face the cart and put the block on with F. You can carry anything with an orange handle this way, anywhere on the island. We painted those handles on ourselves.\n\nEach test uses one of the station's prototype charges. When they run out, the station has to recharge, which takes a while, so measure before you commit.",
            investigate: "Use the ruler (R). Click the cart's starting point, then the centre of the marker (yellow pins mark both), and note the horizontal distance. It goes into the notebook (N) under a short name such as d1. Enter that name, or the number with its unit, in the blueprint below. If you like, try the number without a unit first. The station will refuse it and tell you why, and it won't use a charge.",
            concepts: ["units"],
            requires: [],
            charges: 3,
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.1",
            kind: Chase,
            title: "Catch the crawler",
            task: "Send the haul cart to reach the crawler on the transfer marker.",
            why: "That crawler is one of theirs, still walking its rounds after all this time, and it's carrying the sight on its back. Their launchers aim with that. Without it, nothing here can aim at all.",
            done: "The sight's on the crane, and the light ran on up the road to the tower. The launchers can aim now. They just need to know how hard this planet pulls.",
            brief: "One of the builders' crawlers still walks the haul track, round after round, as it may have done for a very long time. On its back it carries the sight, the piece the launchers further up the road aim with. At the end of each round it waits at its mark, then sets off again at one steady speed. Nothing here can stop or steer it.\n\nThe cart that carried the block is waiting behind it in the same rail, past the stone gate. From here on it's the haul cart, and it runs at whatever speed you set. When it reaches the crawler the two couple together, and only the crane at the transfer marker can lift the sight off. Set the cart's speed so that it reaches the crawler right on the marker.\n\nWhen you commit, the crawler sets off on its next round and the cart leaves its mark at the same moment.",
            investigate: "Use the ruler (R). Click the haul cart's front coupler, then the crawler's rear coupler, to get the crawler's head start. Then click the cart's front coupler and the centre of the transfer marker. The cart reaches the crawler when the two couplers meet. For the distance between the two timing posts beside the track, click the tops of both.\n\nEvery run of the experiment below times the crawler with the stopwatch, from the first timing post to the second, and notes the time (for example as t1). The crawler's speed isn't written anywhere, so you'll have to work it out from what you measure.",
            concepts: ["velocity", "relative_velocity"],
            requires: ["1.1.0"],
            charges: 3,
            demo: Some(DemoDef(
                question: "The crawler and the haul cart leave their marks at the same moment, and the cart's steady speed is the lower one. A flash marks both of them at equal moments. How does the gap between them change?",
                choices: [
                    "It grows by the same amount every moment.",
                    "It grows faster and faster.",
                    "It stays the same, because both are moving.",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                reveal: "The gap grows by the same amount every moment. They each cover equal distances in equal times, and the crawler covers more, so the gap grows at the difference of their speeds. If the cart were the faster one, it would close the gap at that difference instead.",
            )),
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.2",
            kind: Gravity,
            title: "Local gravity",
            task: "Measure this planet's gravitational acceleration.",
            why: "Their launchers throw exactly as hard as they're told, so before we can tell them anything we need to know how hard this planet pulls. All I've got are the tables from home. I'd much rather trust your number than my guess.",
            done: "I've got your g now, and the tower lit up all the way to the top. The sight's going up to the ledge launcher, and every shot from here on will be worked out with your number.",
            brief: "The launchers do what they're told and don't work anything out for themselves. Before they can aim, we need the gravitational acceleration here, and the only value I carry is the one from home.\n\nThe builders' drop tower beside this station holds a test mass. Measure how far it falls and how long the fall takes. When you commit a value, a second mass is released from the taller survey mast and timed against your prediction.",
            investigate: "Use the ruler (R) to click the release point at the top of the drop tower, then the landing plate below it. The stopwatch times the fall on every run of the experiment below and notes it.\n\nThere's also a light gate on the stand by this station. You can carry it (F) and clamp it to the drop tower at any of the orange notches painted on it a metre apart, from a metre below the release point down to the plate. Point at the height you want on the tower and press F. With a gate fitted, each run times the fall from the release point to the gate, and notes the drop and the time as a pair sharing one number (such as h2 and t2). The stopwatch reads to hundredths of a second, so a short drop only gives a rough value. The longer the drop, the better the reading.",
            concepts: ["acceleration", "free_fall"],
            requires: ["1.1.1"],
            charges: 3,
            demo: Some(DemoDef(
                question: "The test mass is released from the top of the drop tower. A flash marks where it is at equal moments as it falls. How will the marks be spaced?",
                choices: [
                    "Evenly, because it falls at a steady speed.",
                    "Further apart lower down, because it keeps speeding up.",
                    "Closer together lower down, because it slows as it falls.",
                ],
                answer: 1,
                reveal: "The gaps grow all the way down. In each moment the mass falls further than in the moment before, because it gains speed at a steady rate, and that rate is the planet's gravitational acceleration.",
            )),
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.3",
            kind: Canyon,
            title: "Across the canyon",
            task: "Land the helm's casket on the platform across the canyon.",
            why: "Now the helm itself has to go across the canyon in its casket, over to the tilting launcher. It's our first real shot with your g, and I'll admit I'm a little nervous.",
            done: "It made it across, and both rims lit up! The tiller's on the tilting launcher now, pointing at the ridge.",
            brief: "The skiff's helm is a long stone tiller, packed in one of the builders' own caskets, and it has to cross the canyon. Its launcher sits on the canyon's high rim and fires north, horizontally. The casket has to land on the orange strip on the far side, beside the tilting launcher. The strip is only one block long, so you'll need to work the shot out properly.\n\nThe launcher's speed dial only turns within a band, either from wear or because it was built that way. We found its stops and painted them on the rating plate. From the bare ledge, no speed in that band reaches the strip. The launcher rides in a stone jack frame at the rim, with sockets that take the dressed blocks lying on the ledge, and every block you slide in raises it by one metre.\n\nWork out how high the launcher has to stand for a speed in the band to reach the strip, and build it up to that height. Pick up a block on the ledge with F, carry it to the frame and put it in with F (pressing F at the frame takes one back out). Then set the speed for the height you built, and commit.",
            investigate: "Use the ruler (R) to click the launcher's release point, then the centre of the strip. The ruler reports the horizontal and the vertical separation. Remember that the release point rises with the launcher. The rating plate on the station gives the dial's band, so note that too.",
            concepts: ["free_fall", "independence"],
            requires: ["1.1.2"],
            charges: 3,
            demo: Some(DemoDef(
                question: "Two test blocks leave the ledge at the same moment. One is simply dropped, and the other is fired straight out sideways. Which reaches the canyon floor first?",
                choices: [
                    "The dropped block.",
                    "The fired block.",
                    "Both at the same moment.",
                ],
                answer: 2,
                reveal: "They land together. Firing a block sideways gives it no downward speed, so it falls just like the dropped one while it travels across.",
            )),
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.4",
            kind: Ridge,
            title: "Over the ridge",
            task: "Launch the tiller over the ridge into the landing circle on the quay.",
            why: "This is the one that matters. The helm made it across the canyon, and now this launcher throws it over the ridge onto the old quay, right beside the skiff.",
            done: "The tiller's landed in the circle, and the gate on the quay has woken up. The skiff took its helm, and the helm lit up!",
            brief: "The landing circle is cut into an old quay beyond the ridge, on level ground on the north shore, beside the skiff's slipway. This launcher can tilt, but it has a rated maximum speed and will refuse anything faster.\n\nThe tiller has to pass over the ridge and come down in the circle. Set the launch angle and the launch speed. Leave some room to spare over the ridge, because the tiller isn't a point.",
            investigate: "Use the ruler (R) to measure from the release point to the centre of the circle, and to the top of the ridge on the side facing you.",
            concepts: ["components", "range", "independence"],
            requires: ["1.1.3"],
            charges: 3,
            demo: Some(DemoDef(
                question: "Two test slugs are fired from the tilting launcher at the same speed, one at 30 degrees and one at 60 degrees. Which lands further away?",
                choices: [
                    "The 30 degree shot.",
                    "The 60 degree shot.",
                    "They land in the same place.",
                ],
                answer: 2,
                reveal: "They land in the same place. The steep shot climbs higher and stays up longer, but it covers less ground each second, while the flat one is quicker and comes down sooner. A flat angle and a steep angle can share the same range.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
